ying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(3{y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-2{x}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\ & ={∬}_{D}(-6{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,drd\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-6{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(-6\\cdot (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-9\\cdot (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={∬}_{Δ}(-6{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -9{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={∬}_{Δ}(-6{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -9{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}-6{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -9{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536065 17592186045443 true] [17592186563829 1080 "- 3 r^{3} \\cos^{2}{(\\theta )}" 13194139536065 17592186045443 true] [17592186563829 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536065 17592186045443 true] [17592186563829 1083 #uuid "333ff705-2b09-5376-86d1-0978a1ff962e" 13194139536065 17592186045443 true] [17592186563830 1080 "9 r^{2} (\\sin^{2}{(\\theta )} - 2)" 13194139536065 17592186045443 true] [17592186563830 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536065 17592186045443 true] [17592186563830 1083 #uuid "367d7cc1-3324-54fc-9fb9-97d117004d5f" 13194139536065 17592186045443 true] [17592186563831 1080 "- 12 r^{3} \\sin^{2}{(\\theta )}" 13194139536065 17592186045443 true] [17592186563831 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536065 17592186045443 true] [17592186563831 1083 #uuid "36bc7de8-7904-5380-a4ad-90310a70debf" 1